# Contributing to {{ title }}

## The gate

```console
$ cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo test
$ pnpm install && pnpm lint && pnpm test && pnpm build
```

The same commands CI runs, so a green terminal means a green pull request.

## The two halves

A Tauri plugin is a Rust crate the application registers and an npm package the
webview calls, and they only work if they agree on three spellings: the plugin
name, the command key, and the permission generated from it. All three are
declared once here - `NAME` in `src/lib.rs`, `COMMANDS` in `build.rs`, and the
`plugin:{{ name }}|{{ command_key }}` string in `guest-js/index.ts` - and the
tests on both sides hold them to each other.

They are published from one repository for the same reason: a release that
moved one half and not the other would fail in the webview, at run time, with a
permission error rather than a build failure.

## Layout

| Path | What lives there |
| --- | --- |
| `src/lib.rs` | The plugin, its state and the builder that registers it |
| `src/commands.rs` | The surface the webview may call |
| `build.rs` | The command list Tauri generates permissions from |
| `permissions/` | The permission set an application grants |
| `guest-js/` | The npm package the webview imports |
| `assets/` | The mark's three master SVGs |
| `docs/adr/` | Architecture decisions, newest last |
